3.-using-plagues/2.-creating-an-app.html.description:
An app is a consumer. It calls capabilities through the Manager and never touches a worker. This is the easy half — the whole secure transport is one helper.A plain console/exe that references XRUIOS.Interfaces. That's all — an app never references Eclipse, Notary, or a worker directly.&lt;Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk"&gt; &lt;PropertyGroup&gt; &lt;OutputType&gt;Exe&lt;/OutputType&gt; &lt;TargetFramework&gt;net9.0&lt;/TargetFramework&gt; &lt;Nullable&gt;enable&lt;/Nullable&gt; &lt;ImplicitUsings&gt;enable&lt;/ImplicitUsings&gt; &lt;/PropertyGroup&gt; &lt;ItemGroup&gt; &lt;ProjectReference Include="..\XRUIOS.Interfaces\XRUIOS.Interfaces.csproj" /&gt; &lt;/ItemGroup&gt;
&lt;/Project&gt;
An app needs its appId, per-app PSK, and the broker address. There are two ways to receive them, and the app can support both:using XRUIOS.Interfaces; // (a) env handoff — if the Manager SPAWNED us, our creds are already in the environment.
string? appId = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("XRUIOS_APP_ID");
string? pskB64 = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("XRUIOS_APP_PSK");
string? broker = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("XRUIOS_BROKER_ADDR"); // (b) self-enroll — if we're our own exe, ask the Manager. It ATTESTS our binary
// (verifies we're the registered app) before handing anything back.
if (appId is null || pskB64 is null || broker is null)
{ var prov = await XruiosEnrollment.EnrollAsync("myapp"); // throws if attestation fails appId = prov.AppId; pskB64 = prov.PskBase64; broker = prov.BrokerAddress;
}
byte[] appPsk = Convert.FromBase64String(pskB64);
Why two ways? Env handoff is what you get when the Manager launches you (or Manager run myapp). Self-enrollment lets your app be its own standalone exe — double-click it and it enrolls itself. Both leave no secret at rest: env is parent→child, enrollment is over an attested local pipe. See <a data-href="2 - Attestation and Enrollment" href=".html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">2 - Attestation and Enrollment</a>.Open one EclipseSecureClient for the app's lifetime and reuse it — don't reconnect per call.await using var xruios = await EclipseSecureClient.ConnectAsync( serverAddress: broker, clientName: appId, psk: appPsk, identity: appId); // stable id — the Manager keys permissions on it // Call a capability by name. The Manager routes it to the owning worker after a permission check.
string forecast = await xruios.InvokeAsync&lt;string&gt;( "GetForecast", new Dictionary&lt;string, object?&gt; { ["city"] = "Höllvania" }); Console.WriteLine(forecast);
If your app wasn't granted a capability, the call throws with the Manager's message. Catch it:try
{ await xruios.InvokeAsync&lt;string&gt;("DeleteAllEvents", new() { ["confirm"] = "yes" });
}
catch (Exception ex)
{ // "Worker call failed: XRUIOS.Permission denied 'DeleteAllEvents' for app:myapp." Console.Error.WriteLine(ex.Message);
}
The refusal is enforced at the Manager, before anything reaches a worker. A denied app learns nothing about the worker — not even that the capability exists.using XRUIOS.Interfaces; string? appId = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("XRUIOS_APP_ID");
string? pskB64 = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("XRUIOS_APP_PSK");
string? broker = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("XRUIOS_BROKER_ADDR");
if (appId is null || pskB64 is null || broker is null)
{ var prov = await XruiosEnrollment.EnrollAsync("myapp"); appId = prov.AppId; pskB64 = prov.PskBase64; broker = prov.BrokerAddress;
} await using var xruios = await EclipseSecureClient.ConnectAsync( broker, appId, Convert.FromBase64String(pskB64), identity: appId); var forecast = await xruios.InvokeAsync&lt;string&gt;("GetForecast", new() { ["city"] = "Höllvania" });
Console.WriteLine(forecast);
<br>You still have to register the app with the Manager — mint its creds, grant it capabilities, and (for self-enroll) put its exe in the catalog. See <a data-href="4 - Registering and Launching" href=".html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">4 - Registering and Launching</a>. An unregistered app gets "no such registered app" on enrollment, and an ungranted capability is refused.

3.-using-plagues/4.-registering-and-laun.description:
Workers and apps are inert until the Manager knows about them. The Manager is where you declare what exists, mint credentials, and grant permissions. This page is the Manager side of the two previous ones.The Manager launches workers as supervised children. In the Manager's startup, add your worker's exe to the list it resolves and start it:// The supervisor checksums the folder, launches with the per-worker PSK + Manager public key,
// waits for the worker's announcement, and records it in the registry.
var worker = new WorkerDescriptor
{ Name = "MyCompany.WeatherWorker", // must equal the worker's serverName ExecutablePath = pathToWeatherWorkerExe
};
await supervisor.StartAsync(worker);
What StartAsync does:
Notary-checksums the worker's folder — refuses to launch a tampered worker.
Mints a fresh per-worker PSK.
Launches the exe with env XRUIOS_MANAGER_MANAGED=1, XRUIOS_WORKER_PSK=&lt;psk&gt;, XRUIOS_MANAGER_PUBKEY=&lt;manager public key&gt;.
Waits for the worker's WorkerAnnouncement (matched by pid) and records {address, capabilities, public key, PSK}.
Hooks process exit for auto-restart — if the worker dies or trips its tamper guard, it re-checksums and relaunches on a fresh port.
The Manager holds the per-worker PSK and its own private identity key. When the broker relays a call, it signs the handshake with that private key; the worker verifies with the public key it was pinned. A compromised worker holds only the public key and can't impersonate the Manager to any other worker.An app needs three things from the Manager: an identity, a password, and its grants.// 1. Mint the app's credentials (appId + a per-app PSK held only in the Manager).
var cred = apps.Register("myapp"); // 2. Grant it each capability it may call. Anything not granted is refused.
await permissions.GrantAsync(cred.AppId, "GetForecast");
// (do NOT grant "DeleteAllEvents" — the broker will refuse it) // 3. Register the exe so the app can self-enroll (or be launched on demand).
launcher.Register("myapp", pathToMyAppExe, cred);
Grants are persisted in XRUIOS.Permission — an encrypted {appId → {capability: level}} store gated by the Manager's master key. Level 1 = granted; an absent capability is denied.Three ways, in increasing independence:The Manager launches the app as a child with its creds in the environment. Nothing on disk.A shortcut/icon runs XRUIOS.Manager run myapp, which asks the running Manager (over a local control pipe) to spawn the registered app. The launcher process never sees a secret.XRUIOS.Manager run myapp
The app is its own exe. Run it directly; it calls XruiosEnrollment.EnrollAsync("myapp"), the Manager verifies its binary (it must be the registered exe at its registered path, and Notary-clean), then returns the creds. See <a data-href="2 - Attestation and Enrollment" href=".html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">2 - Attestation and Enrollment</a>.All three leave no secret at rest. The difference is only who starts the process; the credential is always handed over live (env or attested pipe), never written to disk.When the app calls a capability, the broker's router does this, in order:If the permission check says no, the broker returns a refusal and never contacts the worker.
